      "math_academy_id": "q-126105",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(2n-1)!}(x-2)^{2n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(2n-1)!}(x-2)^{2n}=-(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{120}(x-2)^{6}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{20}(x-2)^{5}+⋯.\n$$",
